    "passage": "Scams are getting more and more sophisticated. Scammers keep up to date with things that are in the news, and use government schemes people might have heard of but aren’t familiar with to create new scams. The tactics used by scammers and fraudsters can [[1]] from someone knocking on your door to a nuisance phone call. In recent years, the Internet and advances in digital communications have made it easier for scammers to [[2]] you and steal information. If you happen to find yourself in the following situations, you may have already [[3]] the most common types of scams. One of them is spam emails pretending to be from your bank. Another is texts claiming that [[4]] is required for a package to be delivered. As for the “mum and dad” scam, scammers using WhatsApp pose as family members in order to [[5]] victims into transferring money.\nKnowing what to [[6]] when it comes to scams is one of the best ways to protect yourself. Here are some tips. Do not open emails from people you don’t know or click on any links in an email or a message unless you are sure it is safe. Scammers often use a [[7]] variation in spelling or logo to lure you. In addition, secure your personal information. Never give out personal information. This can be used to steal your [[8]] and access accounts. In particular, you should never share your full PIN or password with anyone. Speaking of passwords, use strong ones, for they are [[9]] to online security. Also, keep your software up to date and install software that provides antivirus, firewall, and email filter services. Finally, stay [[10]] of the latest cyber threats and learn how to avoid them. You can never be too careful when it comes to protecting yourself and your finances.",
